  "preamble": "Deglottalization of the bilabial ejective [pʼ] to a plain voiceless stop. Russian has no ejectives and renders Georgian პ [pʼ] with п [p], just as კ [kʼ] and ტ [tʼ] become к and т. The same words show Georgian ჭ [t͡ʃʼ] becoming Russian ч [t͡ɕ], a different target.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deglottalization, Loanword Adaptation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Georgian loans in Russian",
      "languageFamily": "East Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Georgian ხაჭაპური [χät͡ʃʼäpʼuɾi] 'khachapuri, cheese bread'",
          "to": "Russian хачапури [xət͡ɕɪˈpurʲɪ]"
        },
        {
          "from": "Georgian კუპატი [kʼupʼätʼi] (a sausage)",
          "to": "Russian купаты",
          "note": "All three ejectives lose their glottalization: kʼ > k, pʼ > p, tʼ > t. The Georgian entry lists купаты as a borrowed descendant."
        }
      ]
    }
